Descriptor plumbing beyond plain reads and writes: pipes, waiting on several descriptors (poll,
epoll), wakeup counters, scatter and gather I/O, terminals.

| Function | Does |
|---|---|
| `sys::pipe(flags)` | a new pipe, as `pipe_ends` (`read_end()`, `write_end()`); flags from `ca::fs` |
| `sys::dup2(fd, target)` | makes target a copy of fd (the copy is not close-on-exec) |
| `sys::poll(entries, timeout_ms)` | waits for events on `poll_entry`s; -1 waits forever, 0 not at all |
| `sys::epoll_create(flags)` | a new epoll descriptor (Linux) |
| `sys::epoll_add(epfd, fd, events, data)`, `epoll_modify`, `epoll_remove(epfd, fd)` | what epfd watches: `epoll_events` bits, a 64-bit word handed back with each report |
| `sys::epoll_wait(epfd, reported, timeout_ns)` | fills `epoll_event`s (`events()`, `data()`, `has(bits)`); nanosecond timeout, -1 forever |
| `sys::eventfd(initial, flags)` | a counter descriptor, readable while nonzero: one thread wakes another's wait |
| `sys::eventfd_write(fd, value)`, `sys::eventfd_read(fd)` | adds to the counter, takes it whole |
| `sys::readv(fd, buffers)`, `sys::writev(fd, buffers)` | one call over several `io_buffer`s |
| `sys::isatty(fd)` | whether fd is a terminal |
| `sys::get_terminal_size(fd)` | rows and columns of the terminal fd is attached to |
| `sys::tcgetattr(fd)` | the terminal's settings, as `terminal_settings`; `not_a_terminal` otherwise |
| `sys::tcsetattr(fd, settings, when)` | applies settings `now` (the default), after a `drain`, or with a `flush` of unread input |
| `sys::enter_raw_mode(fd)` | puts the terminal in raw mode; returns the settings it had, to restore |
| `sys::tcgetpgrp(fd)`, `sys::tcsetpgrp(fd, group)` | the foreground process group of the controlling terminal, read or given |

`terminal_settings` is struct termios without `<termios.h>`: the flag words `input()`,
`output()`, `control()`, `local()` with their `set_` counterparts, bits from
`sys::terminal_input`, `terminal_output`, `terminal_control` and `terminal_local`, and the
control characters by slot (`character(slot)`, `set_character(slot, value)`, slots in
`sys::terminal_character`). `raw()` is a copy in raw mode, as cfmakeraw makes it. On Linux the
settings have the kernel's layout and go to ioctl as they are.

```c++
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/io.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto previous = ca::sys::enter_raw_mode(0);
	if (!previous)
		return previous.error() == ca::errc::not_a_terminal ? 0 : 1;
	auto raw = ca::sys::tcgetattr(0);
	if (!raw || (raw->local() & ca::sys::terminal_local::echo) != 0U)
		return 2;
	return ca::sys::tcsetattr(0, *previous) ? 0 : 3;
}
```

`poll_events` holds the event bits without `<poll.h>`: `readable`, `writable`, `urgent`, and the
reported-only `error`, `hangup` and `invalid`. A `poll_entry` records the events asked for and,
after the call, those that occurred (`reported()`, `has(bits)`).

```c++
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/file.hpp"
#include "ca/fs.hpp"
#include "ca/io.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto ends = ca::sys::pipe(ca::fs::cloexec_t::OPEN_VALUE | ca::fs::nonblock_t::OPEN_VALUE);
	if (!ends)
		return 1;

	ca::sys::poll_entry watch[1] = {ca::sys::poll_entry{ends->read_end(), ca::sys::poll_events::readable}};
	auto idle = ca::sys::poll(watch, 0);
	if (!idle || *idle != 0U)
		return 2;

	const ca::sys::io_buffer parts[] = {ca::sv::str{"hello, "}, ca::sv::str{"world"}};
	auto sent = ca::sys::writev(ends->write_end(), parts);
	if (!sent || *sent != 12U)
		return 3;

	auto ready = ca::sys::poll(watch, 1000);
	if (!ready || *ready != 1U || !watch[0].has(ca::sys::poll_events::readable))
		return 4;
	char got[16]{};
	auto read = ca::sys::read(ends->read_end(), got);
	if (!read || ca::sv::str{got, *read} != ca::sv::str{"hello, world"})
		return 5;

	auto empty = ca::sys::read(ends->read_end(), got);
	if (empty || empty.error() != ca::errc::try_again)
		return 6;
	if (ca::sys::isatty(ends->read_end()))
		return 7;
	return ca::sys::close(ends->read_end()) && ca::sys::close(ends->write_end()) ? 0 : 8;
}
```

An `io_buffer` has the layout of `struct iovec`, so an array of them goes to the kernel as is,
and it converts from anything a byte span is made from: text, char arrays, strings. The caller
closes both ends of a pipe.

### epoll

`poll` takes the whole set on every call; epoll keeps it in the kernel, so a wait costs what is
ready, not what is watched. Each registration carries a 64-bit word (an index, a pointer)
handed back with its reports. `epoll_events` holds the bits: `readable`, `writable`, `urgent`,
`peer_closed`, the always-reported `error` and `hangup`, and the modes `edge` (report changes
only: drain until `try_again`), `oneshot` (report once, then rearm with `epoll_modify`) and
`exclusive`. An `epoll_event` has the kernel's layout, packed on x86-64, so `epoll_wait` fills an
array in place; its timeout is in nanoseconds (epoll_pwait2, milliseconds rounded up on kernels
without it). An `eventfd` counter makes waits wakeable: another thread `eventfd_write`s, the
waiter's epoll reports it readable, `eventfd_read` resets it. `ca::io::reactor` (layer 6) is an
event loop over both. Linux only: `errc::not_supported` elsewhere.

```c++
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/file.hpp"
#include "ca/fs.hpp"
#include "ca/io.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	const int flags = ca::fs::cloexec_t::OPEN_VALUE | ca::fs::nonblock_t::OPEN_VALUE;
	auto ep = ca::sys::epoll_create(ca::fs::cloexec_t::OPEN_VALUE);
	auto ends = ca::sys::pipe(flags);
	auto wake = ca::sys::eventfd(0U, flags);
	if (!ep || !ends || !wake)
		return 1;
	if (!ca::sys::epoll_add(*ep, ends->read_end(), ca::sys::epoll_events::readable, 1U)
	 || !ca::sys::epoll_add(*ep, *wake, ca::sys::epoll_events::readable, 2U))
		return 2;
	if (!ca::sys::write(ends->write_end(), ca::sv::str{"x"}) || !ca::sys::eventfd_write(*wake, 1U))
		return 3;
	ca::sys::epoll_event reported[4];
	auto ready = ca::sys::epoll_wait(*ep, reported, 1000000000);
	if (!ready || *ready != 2U || reported[0].data() + reported[1].data() != 3U)
		return 4;
	auto count = ca::sys::eventfd_read(*wake);
	if (!count || *count != 1U)
		return 5;
	auto idle = ca::sys::epoll_wait(*ep, reported, 1000000);
	if (!idle || *idle != 1U || reported[0].data() != 1U)
		return 6;
	return ca::sys::close(*wake) && ca::sys::close(ends->read_end()) && ca::sys::close(ends->write_end())
		&& ca::sys::close(*ep) ? 0 : 7;
}
```
